# Stamp the build commit + date into version.txt when `git archive` makes the relay bundle.
# (export-subst substitutes the $Format$ placeholders only in the archive, not the working tree.)
version.txt export-subst

# ── the public bundle carries no internal documentation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AUDIT-2026-07-28 F2. /relay-app/bundle.tgz is public on purpose — the self-host installer curls it and
# every relay's self-update pulls it — and it is a `git archive` of the release ref, so it carried every
# tracked file regardless of the static handler's denylist. Measured on the release funnel: HTTP 200,
# 54,055,346 bytes, no auth, 61 markdown files including HANDOFF.md, AUDIT-BACKLOG.md,
# AUDIT-2026-07-26-RECOVERY.md and DEVICE-TEST-CHECKLIST.md. Those name live vulnerabilities and the dates
# they were introduced: the cheapest, highest-value thing on the box for an attacker.
#
# The morning's fix named the STATIC ROUTE only (DENY_DIR, scripts/gateway.mjs ~3196) and the bundle route
# (~2386, 800 lines earlier) walked straight past it. export-ignore is the one place that covers every
# producer at once: build-strict-tgz.sh (what the release host actually serves), ensureSignedBundle's raw
# `git archive` fallback, the un-cached streaming route that never touches the cache, and build-pages.sh.
# Because ensureSignedBundle signs the bytes it has just produced, the release signature covers the
# filtered bundle with no other change.
#
# This stops the documents SHIPPING. It does not remove them from a relay that already pulled an older
# bundle — relay-update.sh untars OVER the install and deletes nothing — so the static denylist remains
# the thing that stops those copies being served. Guarded by scripts/bundle-contents.test.mjs, which
# builds the real tarballs and lists them rather than reasoning about the rules.
#
# LICENSE has no extension and still ships, deliberately.
docs        export-ignore
reference   export-ignore
*.md        export-ignore

# AUDIT-2026-07-28 F3/F4. The same files the relay now REFUSES TO SERVE were still being shipped to every
# church's box inside the bundle, and into the Cloudflare Pages build, because both start from this same
# archive. Not serving a file you have already copied onto someone's disk is half a fix.
#   deploy/  — THIS box's systemd units: the install path, the service account name, the exact Node
#              version. A self-hoster never needs them: relay-app/install.sh writes its own unit files
#              inline (`cat > /etc/systemd/system/$SVC.service`), it does not copy these.
#   ci/      — build/CI job descriptors. Read by .github/workflows from a git checkout, never from a bundle.
deploy      export-ignore
ci          export-ignore

# ── ARCHITECTURE-AUDIT-2026-07-30 A5 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The rules above stopped the DOCUMENTS shipping. They did not stop the test suite shipping, and 52 of the 92
# `*.test.mjs` files carry the same content the documents did: the audit finding IDs, what each hole was, when
# it was introduced, and in several cases what is still only half fixed —
#   AUDIT-2026-07-20 C2/E1 · 07-24 FINDINGS · 07-26 CRITICAL/S5 · 07-28 F1 F2 F3 F6 F7 F9 F10 F11 F12 F14 F15
#   F18 F19 · 07-29 S1 S2
# Withdrawing AUDIT-2026-07-28-READONLY.md while shipping 52 files that narrate F1-F19 is not a reduction in
# what an attacker learns. A relay runs gateway.mjs; it has never needed the suite that tests it.
#
#   capacitor.config.json — states in four lines that every shipped Android build has
#                           `webContentsDebuggingEnabled: true`. That is a KNOWN open item (it is on for pilot
#                           diagnosis and must be off before go-live) and the bundle was advertising it. The
#                           static handler already refuses this file for exactly this reason; the bundle was
#                           undoing that.
#   .github/              — workflow definitions. Read by GitHub from a checkout, never from a bundle.
#   relay-app/desktop/    — the Suite's Tauri build tree (Cargo.toml, build.rs, main.rs). The control UI beside
#                           it is deliberately public; its BUILD SOURCES are not part of that.
#
# NOT excluded, deliberately, and each is a decision rather than an oversight:
#   package.json      — the relay's own manifest; bundle-contents' MUST_SHIP asserts it, and the box needs it.
#   package-lock.json — left IN for now. It fingerprints every dependency version, and the static handler does
#                       refuse to serve it — but relay-update.sh's comment names lockfile-pinned installs as a
#                       planned follow-up, and that would need this file present in the bundle. Removing it is
#                       the owner's call, not a side effect of widening a test.
*.test.mjs             export-ignore
*.probe.mjs            export-ignore
capacitor.config.json  export-ignore
.github                export-ignore
relay-app/desktop      export-ignore
